---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import StageTrack from "../components/StageTrack.astro";
import StatusMade from "../components/StatusMade.astro";
import { brandAsset } from "../lib/brand";
import { data } from "../lib/export";
import { statusSlug } from "../lib/guide";
import { SITE } from "../../site.config.mjs";
import { STAGE_PATH, STATUS_LABELS, STATUS_MEANINGS, overlayText } from "../lib/status";
import { NO_REPORT, setAbout } from "../lib/trial-page.mjs";
import { listing } from "../lib/trials-listing";

const codes = Object.keys(STATUS_LABELS) as (keyof typeof STATUS_LABELS)[];
---

<Base
  title="How it works"
  description="How a claim's status is set from approved records, what each status means, and what this site does not say."
>
  <h1 class="title">How it works</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Each page tracks one reported breakthrough and shows the records found since it was reported.
    This page explains how the status on a claim page is set and what it does not tell you.
  </p>

  {/*
    The explainer, made in video/. It never plays on its own and nothing of it is fetched
    until the reader presses play. Its records are dated, because a video cannot be
    rebuilt with the site; the pages under it are the current word.
  */}
  <figure class="explainer" data-explainer>
    <video
      controls
      preload="none"
      playsinline
      width="1920"
      height="1080"
      poster={brandAsset("/video/explainer-poster.jpg")}
      aria-describedby="explainer-note"
    >
      {/* Raise the number with each new render, so a returning reader is not shown the old one. */}
      <source src="/video/explainer.mp4?v=3" type="video/mp4" />
      <a href="/video/explainer.mp4">Download the video</a>
    </video>
    <figcaption id="explainer-note">
      A video of 1 minute 52 seconds, with sound and captions, on how this site works. The records
      in it are as the registries showed them in November 2026; the claim pages are kept current.
    </figcaption>
    <details>
      <summary>What the video says</summary>
      <p>
        “New drug to regenerate lost teeth” (Kyoto University, 2021). “Multiplying teeth” (RIKEN,
        2015). “Regrowing Teeth” (Technische Universität Berlin, 2019). Headlines like these travel
        fast. The follow-up rarely does. So, years later: what came of it?
      </p>
      <p>
        {SITE.name} is a public record of what followed reported medical breakthroughs. Each claim
        gets one page: one treatment, for one condition. At the top is where it stands now. The
        video shows one claim, first reported in 2021, as it stood in November 2026.
      </p>
      <p>
        Underneath are the dated records that say so. Every record links to the registry that holds
        it, so you can check it yourself. And what was promised sits beside what was recorded.
      </p>
      <p>
        The status is set by fixed rules, from public registry records. No editor decides it, and no
        AI model does either. When nothing new has been recorded for years, the page says exactly
        that. That describes the records. It is not a verdict on the treatment.
      </p>
      <p>
        Seen a headline shared again? Paste the article's link into the box on the home page. If the
        claim is tracked, the answer comes back at once. The link is compared inside your browser,
        and sent nowhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is also a browser extension, for Chrome and Safari. Open it on an article, and it
        shows the tracked claim's status right there. If the article is not tracked, it can search
        the trial registries live. Those results are marked automatic and unreviewed, and every
        match shows its confidence score.
      </p>
      <p>No medical advice. No verdicts. Only what the records show, and when.</p>
    </details>
  </figure>

  <StatusMade />

  {/* A long page on a phone: its sections, one tap away. */}
  <nav class="contents" aria-label="On this page">
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#how-a-status-is-set">How a status is set</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-each-status-means">What each status means</a></li>
      <li><a href="#no-signal">No signal</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-a-report-said">What a report said</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-a-trial-entry-shows">What a trial entry shows</a></li>
      {listing.trials.length > 0 && <li><a href="#a-trial-with-no-report">A trial with no report</a></li>}
      <li><a href="#who-a-claim-names">Who a claim names</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-this-site-never-says">What this site never says</a></li>
      <li><a href="#where-the-data-comes-from">Where the data comes from</a></li>
      <li><a href="#an-optional-search-of-a-new-report">An optional search of a new report</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ways-to-keep-up">Ways to keep up</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-is-counted">What is counted</a></li>
      {SITE.correctionsUrl && <li><a href="#report-an-error">Report an error</a></li>}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 class="bar" id="how-a-status-is-set">How a status is set</h2>
  <p>
    A status is derived by fixed rules from the approved evidence events listed on the claim page.
    Where no trial is found, the rules also use the kind of study the first reports described.
    No model and no editor decides a status. Every status lists the events that produced it.
  </p>
  <p>The rules are applied in this order, and the first one that fits sets the status.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      If the paper the first news reports were about has a retraction notice, the status is
      “{STATUS_LABELS.retracted}”.
    </li>
    <li>If a regulator has recorded an approval, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.approved}”.</li>
    <li>
      Otherwise, trials that have not been terminated, withdrawn or marked as missing their primary
      endpoint are the ones that count. If any remain, the status follows the furthest one that has
      started: “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_3}”, “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_2}” or “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_1}”.
      If none has started, or none has a phase, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.trial_registered}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      If no trial remains and a person has marked a trial as missing its primary endpoint, with a
      source link, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.failed}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      If no trial remains and every registered trial was terminated or withdrawn, the status is
      “{STATUS_LABELS.terminated}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      With no trial found at all, the status depends on what the first reports described. If it was
      confirmed laboratory or animal work, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.preclinical_only}”.
      A study in people, or an unconfirmed evidence stage, gives “{STATUS_LABELS.no_trial_found}”.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A claim that is still at a stage open to further evidence (“{STATUS_LABELS.preclinical_only}”
    through “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_3}”, and “{STATUS_LABELS.no_trial_found}”) can also carry the
    no-signal note, described below.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-each-status-means">What each status means</h2>
  <dl class="meanings">
    {
      codes.map((code) => (
        <div>
          <dt>
            {STAGE_PATH.includes(code) && <StageTrack code={code} />}
            <a href={`/guide/${statusSlug(code)}`}>{STATUS_LABELS[code]}</a>
          </dt>
          <dd>{STATUS_MEANINGS[code]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>
  <p class="entry-note">
    Each status links to its entry in <a href="/guide">the guide</a>, which says what it rests on
    and what it does not tell you.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="no-signal">No signal</h2>
  <p>
    A claim that has stayed at an open stage for several years, with no new approved record, shows a
    note such as “{overlayText(5)}”. The note means that the most recent record is that old.
    The count starts from the date of that record, or from the date the claim was first reported
    when no record has been found.
  </p>
  <p>
    It says nothing about work that has not been published or registered, and it is not a status.
    A new record removes it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-a-report-said">What a report said</h2>
  <p>
    A claim page can show, before its records, what the news reports said about the treatment.
    An AI model reads each report and lists up to six things it says beyond its timeline: an
    effect, the treatment’s safety, how it acts, who it could help, how it compares with existing
    treatment, or what it could lead to. Each is written in this site’s own words. A statement
    that repeats eight or more of the report’s words in a row, carries a quotation mark or names a
    person is dropped before it is kept, and the report’s text is never stored.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second automatic step then checks each statement against its report and answers one
    question: does the report say this, as firmly as it is put here, say something weaker, or not
    say it? The percentage beside a statement is how sure that check was that the report says it.
    A statement is shown once the check is sure enough; the rest wait unseen until a person looks
    at them. Beside each statement the page says how firmly the report put it, who the report had
    saying it, by role and never by name, and what the report said it rests on.
  </p>
  <p>
    A statement is what a report said, not what is on record, and this site does not say whether
    the report was right. Nothing on the page sets a statement against a trial or a paper, and
    statements never change a status. A statement that misreads its report is removed by a
    person{SITE.correctionsUrl ? <>: <a href="#report-an-error">report an error</a></> : ""}.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-a-trial-entry-shows">What a trial entry shows</h2>
  <p>
    A claim page lists each of its trials once. Each entry gives what the trial's registry record
    states: its phase, how many people it enrolled or plans to enrol, how it was designed, who
    sponsored it, what it set out to measure, and, where the sponsor has posted them, the results
    for its main measures. These facts are read from the record by fixed rules. No editor and no
    AI model reads them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The facts are a selection from the record, unaltered, with the date the record was read and a
    link to the whole of it. Posted results are the numbers the trial's sponsor gave the registry:
    each group's value, and any p value the sponsor posted with it. The site shows the numbers and
    adds no word on what they mean for the treatment. They do not change a claim's status.
  </p>
  <p>
    An entry does not say where a trial takes place or who may take part in it.
  </p>

  {listing.trials.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <h2 class="bar" id="a-trial-with-no-report">A trial with no report</h2>
      <p>
        Most registered trials are never the subject of a news report, so no claim here cites them.
        For {listing.trials.length.toLocaleString("en")} of them this site still has a page:{" "}
        {setAbout(listing.set, listing.records_read)}. Search finds them by registry number, or by
        words of their title, treatment or condition, after any tracked claim.
      </p>
      <p>
        A study's state is the registry's own, as of the day the registry last changed the record,
        and that day is shown beside it. A record can be listed as not finished and not have been
        changed for years: this site does not check a study's state any other way.
      </p>
      <p>
        Such a page is read from the registry's own record when it is opened. It states what the
        record states and nothing more: the study's state, phase, conditions, treatments, size,
        sponsor and dates, whether results have been posted to the registry and when, and a link to
        the record. It reads “{NO_REPORT}” It carries no status, because a status belongs to a
        claim, and no word on how the study turned out. It does not say where a study takes place
        or who may take part in it.
      </p>
    </>
  )}

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-a-paper-entry-shows">What a paper entry shows</h2>
  <p>
    A claim page lists each of its papers once, under "Papers on record": the paper the first
    news reports were about, papers a trial's registry record names, follow-up papers, and
    related research a literature search found. A paper is not a trial record. No paper sets or
    changes a claim's status, with one exception that is a record of its own: a retraction notice
    for the paper first reported.
  </p>
  <p>
    What an entry says of a paper is read from the paper's record in Europe PMC, an index of
    life-science publications, by fixed rules: its title, the journal, the year, the kind of
    publication the index gives it, whether it is open access, and whether the index marks it as
    retracted. The entry shows the date the record was read. No abstract and no author is stored
    or shown, and no editor and no AI model reads the paper. An entry says what kind of
    publication a paper is and never what it concluded.
  </p>
  <p>
    "Europe PMC subject headings" repeats the index's "Humans" and "Animals" tags.
    These can describe sources of laboratory material, including extracted teeth or human
    cells, so they do not establish a study in living people or animals. A recent paper may
    carry neither heading yet, in which case the entry says nothing. A claim page counts
    trial records and papers separately. These are counts of records, not independent
    studies or participants, and say nothing about whether a treatment works.
  </p>
  <p>
    A paper reaches a trial's entry in one of two ways, both taken from the trial's own registry
    record with no search. "Listed by the sponsor" means the trial's sponsor gave the registry
    the paper and marked it as reporting results from the study. "Linked by the registry" means
    ClinicalTrials.gov filled the paper in from PubMed; the registry itself says such a paper
    "may or may not be about the study". Papers a sponsor listed only as background are not
    shown.
  </p>
  <p>
    Related research is found by searching OpenAlex, an index of scholarly papers, for the
    treatment and the condition. A paper is listed when its match scores in the high band, with
    the score and the reasons a fixed rule found: that its title names the treatment, that it
    names the condition, that it comes from an institution the report names, or that it was
    published on or before the day of the first report. The score is about the match, not about
    the treatment, and a related paper is not counted in the status.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="background-on-a-claims-terms">Background on a claim’s terms</h2>
  <p>
    Near the top of each claim page, a section called At a glance names the treatment and the
    condition the claim is for. Where an open vocabulary has a term for them, it says what they
    are, in the words of an open vocabulary that libraries use to
    index medical research. The definition is the vocabulary publisher’s, shown unchanged and
    marked as theirs, with the edition it came from and the date the record was read. The site
    writes no explanation of a condition or a treatment of its own.
  </p>
  <p>
    A term becomes a candidate by fixed rules only: the trial registry files at least half of the
    claim’s trials under it, or its label is the same as a name the claim uses. An AI model then
    reads each candidate beside the claim and answers one question: is this term the claim’s
    condition (or treatment), a broader term, or something else. The score on a card is how sure
    that answer was. A term scored high is shown, one read as something else is not, and a card
    says when its term is broader than the claim’s. The reasons a term was a candidate are one
    press away. A wrong card is removed by a person.
  </p>
  <p>
    A name the claim uses also counts when the vocabulary files it as another wording of one of
    its terms. Many treatments have no term of their own: a device or an operation described in
    a report’s words, or a compound known only by a code. For those, the card is for the kind of
    treatment it is, such as a monoclonal antibody, an implant or a surgical procedure. The kind
    is chosen by an AI model from a fixed list of kinds that this site keeps, or none of them.
    Each kind on the list is tied to one vocabulary term, whose definition the card shows, and
    the card says it is about the kind of treatment and not the treatment itself. When the trial
    registry lists the treatment under one of its own types, the card gives that as a reason; the
    registry’s type never decides the kind. A treatment that fits no kind on the list has no card.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some treatments are named after a gene or the protein it makes, such as an antibody against a
    named receptor. When a word in the treatment’s name is the same as a human gene’s symbol, or
    another name a gene database lists for it, that gene is a candidate, and an AI model reads it
    beside the claim: does the word mean this gene here, or something else, such as the
    abbreviation of a procedure. A gene scored high gets a card with its name and a description
    of what its product does, in UniProt’s words as Open Targets Platform gives them. The card
    means the report names the gene. It does not mean the treatment is known to work through it,
    and the site states no mechanism.
  </p>
  <p>
    A named drug can also have a card saying what kind of molecule it is and what it acts on.
    These come from the drug database ChEMBL, as Open Targets Platform gives them, and only when
    the database’s own name for a drug is the same as a name the claim uses. That is a rule
    anyone can check, so the card shows the rule and no percentage. If a claim’s names match
    several different drugs, as a class of drugs does, there is no drug card. When a drug’s
    record names exactly one protein as its target, the target card is for that protein and
    comes before any gene the treatment’s name mentions. The card never says how far a drug has
    got, what it is approved for or what it is used for: the records on the page are the source
    for that. ChEMBL’s material is offered under a share-alike licence, so it is shown on the
    page with its credit and is left out of the data download, which says so.
  </p>
  <p>
    A card describes a term. It is not about a claim’s evidence, it never changes a status, and it
    is not medical advice. A claim whose terms are in no vocabulary shows its treatment and condition at a glance with no
    card.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="who-a-claim-names">Who a claim names</h2>
  <p>
    A claim page can list, under "Organisations named", the universities, hospitals and
    companies its sources name, each once. An organisation is listed for one of two reasons,
    and its entry says which: a news report named it, or a trial's registry record names it as
    the trial's lead sponsor, with a link to each of those trials on the page. The entry states
    that part and nothing more. It does not say that an organisation did the work, paid for it,
    stands behind the claim or approves of it. A trial sponsor that is a person is never listed,
    and no researcher, author or other person is named in the section.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each name is looked up in the Research Organization Registry (ROR), an open registry of
    research organisations. A fixed rule accepts ROR's answer only when ROR marks one of its
    records as its chosen answer for the name and the name, ignoring capitals and punctuation,
    is exactly one of that record's own names. A one-word name must match letter for letter, a
    public body's name must state its country, since such names repeat from country to
    country, and a name that is a journal's is never accepted. Then the entry shows what the
    record says: the
    registry's name for the organisation, its kind, its country and its website, with a link to
    the record, ROR's score for how well the name matched, and the date the record was read. No
    AI model takes part. Two names that lead to the same record are one entry. Any other name is
    listed as its source gave it and says it was not matched; no looser match is tried. ROR
    identifies an organisation from its name; it does not verify the organisation's part in a
    claim, and a wrong match is removed by a person.
  </p>
  <p>
    Organisations are listed by their part, reports first and sponsors second, and then by name.
    Nothing is ranked, counted or described: the site gives no measure of an organisation's size
    or standing, and organisations never change a status.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a news report sits on the website of an organisation the claim names, such as a
    university's own news pages, the page says so beside the report: "This report is on" that
    organisation's website. It is found by comparing the report's address with the website in
    the organisation's registry record. The note says where a report is published. It is not a
    judgement on the report, and a report on any other site carries no note of any kind.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-this-site-never-says">What this site never says</h2>
  <p class="plain">
    This site gives no verdict on a claim, a researcher, a company or a news outlet. It gives no
    medical advice and recommends no treatment. A registered clinical trial is not a treatment
    option.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="where-the-data-comes-from">Where the data comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Every record on a claim page links to the source that holds it. Records are shown as the
    registries and databases gave them. The summaries and the wording of statuses are the site’s.
    News articles are linked and summarised; their text is not copied.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      data.sources.map((source) => (
        <li>
          <a href={source.url}>{source.name}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    A treatment reported under a brand name is looked up in
    <a href="https://www.nlm.nih.gov/research/umls/rxnorm/index.html">RxNorm</a> for the name of
    its ingredient, which is then used to search the trial registry. That source asks for the
    statement that follows: “This product uses publicly available data from the
    U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM), National Institutes of Health, Department of Health
    and Human Services; NLM is not responsible for the product and does not endorse or recommend
    this or any other product.”
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="an-optional-search-of-a-new-report">An optional search of a new report</h2>
  <p>
    When you ask for a registry search, OpenAI reads the report's text to identify its treatment
    and condition. We search ClinicalTrials.gov and linked research through Europe PMC, then
    use OpenAI to compare the trial records. This is a live search of those research sources,
    not a general search of the web. Its answer is marked as automatic and unreviewed; it cannot
    create a claim page or change a status. We do not store submitted article text or screenshots.
    Structured answers for independently public reports may be reused for 24 hours; answers
    based only on browser content stay in that browser.
    Read <a href="/privacy">what is sent and how the providers handle it</a> before searching.
  </p>
  <p>
    Related papers come from OpenAlex searches for the treatment and condition. ROR identifies
    organisations explicitly named in the report. These sections open when you need them.
    A paper result does not establish that a treatment works, and an organisation record does
    not verify involvement or endorsement. Neither provider receives the article text, page
    address or screenshot. Public paper and organisation metadata may be cached for seven days.
  </p>
  <p>
    Opening the browser extension checks the current page automatically. A tracked article shows
    its saved claim. After you enable automatic search, an untracked page starts a live registry
    search when the service is available. If its text is insufficient, a screenshot of visible
    content can be sent. Select words first to search only those words, without links or a screenshot.
    Each candidate's match confidence is the model's estimate that the record concerns the reported
    treatment and condition. It is not a verified probability or a rating of treatment effectiveness
    or trial quality. Pages are not searched in the background while the extension is closed.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="ways-to-keep-up">Ways to keep up</h2>
  <p>
    A status can change months or years after a headline. None of these needs an account, and
    none tells us who you are.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Follow a claim.</strong> Press "Follow" on its page. The claim is then listed on
      the home page, with a note when its status changes. The list is kept in your browser
      only.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Since your last visit.</strong> The home page notes the day you open it, in your
      browser only, and next time says what is dated since.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check again later.</strong> A claim page can save a date to your calendar: the
      promised date, six months, a year, or the anniversary of the first report. The file is
      made on your device.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Feeds.</strong> For a feed reader: <a href="/changes.xml">every status change</a>,
      and <a href="/anniversaries.xml">anniversaries of first reports</a>. Each claim page and
      each topic page links a feed of its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>On your own page.</strong> Each claim page gives a sentence to copy, a card to
      post, and a short piece of code that shows the claim's current status on another site.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-is-counted">What is counted</h2>
  <p class="plain">Cloudflare, which hosts this site, counts requests and errors to operate the service. We do not add analytics, set tracking cookies, or keep reader profiles. Request logging for our Worker is disabled.</p>

  {
    /* Said only where a claim page does carry the link. */
    SITE.correctionsUrl && (
      <>
        <h2 class="bar" id="report-an-error">Report an error</h2>
        <p>Each claim page says how to report an error in it.</p>
      </>
    )
  }
</Base>

<script>
  // Once the reader starts the video it is shown at its playing brightness.
  const explainer = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-explainer]");
  explainer?.querySelector("video")?.addEventListener(
    "play",
    () => explainer.setAttribute("data-started", ""),
    { once: true },
  );
</script>
